WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:05.020
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.420
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.280
We were on The Office together, and we're best friends.

4
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.400
And now we're doing the ultimate Office Lovers podcast just for you.

5
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.380
Each week, we will dive deeper into the world of The Office

6
00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:21.060
with exclusive interviews, behind-the-scenes details, and lots of BFF stories.

7
00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.120
We're The Office Lady 6.0.

8
00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:28.550
Oh, hey there.

9
00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:32.110
Today, we're breaking down another webisode, The Mentor.

10
00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:34.790
And we're also going to share mentor stories

11
00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:37.950
from you guys who wrote in, plus some of our own.

12
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:42.530
Yes, but first, before we get started on all of that, can I do a personal share?

13
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.110
Yeah, what's going on?

14
00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:51.610
Do you want the long, medium, or short version of this story?

15
00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:53.370
Actually, just pick medium or short.

16
00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.630
I'll go medium, okay?

17
00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:02.250
So, I got up this morning, and I thought, today is a drawstring pant day.

18
00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.090
Oh, I love those days.

19
00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:08.910
I mean, I've been putting in more effort when we come into the studio.

20
00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:13.770
I only have to work outside of the house one day a week, okay?

21
00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:17.910
Only one day you need to look super cute, unless there's a school thing.

22
00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:18.530
True.

23
00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:19.010
Yeah.

24
00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.530
But today, even though it was not a work-from-home day,

25
00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:26.970
I was like, I just feel so good in my pajama pants.

26
00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:27.990
What can I wear?

27
00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:31.830
But then I took a shower, and after the shower, I thought,

28
00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.470
you know what, make the effort, Jenna.

29
00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:35.730
It's one day a week.

30
00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:36.290
Yeah.

31
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:42.450
Now, I am still wearing a drawstring pant, but I put in an effort, and I thought I'm going to put

32
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:45.710
on the makeup, because by the way, I wasn't going to put on makeup either.

33
00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.110
But when you say drawstring pants, you're not in sweatpants.

34
00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:53.090
These look like sort of a khaki kind of green trouser.

35
00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:54.050
Yeah.

36
00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.570
They are an elastic waist trouser.

37
00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:56.850
Oh.

38
00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:58.290
Well, I'm here for it.

39
00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:58.850
Thank you.

40
00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.710
I mean, it's a happy middle ground.

41
00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:01.110
Okay.

42
00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:03.030
But this isn't even about my pants.

43
00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:03.790
I don't know.

44
00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:09.110
That's the medium part of the story, which was that my instinct, the point of this is my instinct was,

45
00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:12.930
make almost no effort today, and that will make you happy.

46
00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:18.510
And I went against my instinct, and I decided to put on makeup and all the whatever.

47
00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:19.110
Mm-hmm.

48
00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:24.050
And while I was putting on my makeup, I stabbed myself in the eye with my mascara wand.

49
00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:25.210
Oh, God.

50
00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.730
And it still hurts.

51
00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:29.550
It's so painful when that happens.

52
00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:33.690
I hope I don't need to go to the eye doctor after this recording.

53
00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:35.030
I mean, I think I'm okay.

54
00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.630
Can I guess which eye it is looking at you?

55
00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:37.810
Yeah.

56
00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:38.450
Guess which one.

57
00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:39.570
Is it your right eye?

58
00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:39.990
Yes.

59
00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.130
Because it's a little puffy in the corner.

60
00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:42.750
Of course, yes.

61
00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:43.770
It hurts.

62
00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:44.570
Oh, honey.

63
00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:48.790
I am still recovering, and it was a good, like, 90 minutes ago.

64
00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:52.790
I wouldn't have noticed right away, but since you said it,

65
00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.390
now I'm looking at it, and lady, you got yourself good.

66
00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:57.190
I really did.

67
00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:59.570
So I don't know what the moral of that story is,

68
00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:04.530
but I think if you are feeling like it's a low-effort day, just listen to yourself.

69
00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:05.110
Lean into it.

70
00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.630
I wish I just listened to myself.

71
00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:07.970
Why the mascara today?

72
00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:10.290
Clearly, I didn't want it.

73
00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:12.150
You were rejecting it.

74
00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.190
Your whole body was like, no.

75
00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:16.210
So that's it.

76
00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:16.950
That was my share.

77
00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:18.230
I hope it gets better.

78
00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:19.130
Thank you.

79
00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.410
We also had a guest in our studio today.

80
00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:23.230
We did.

81
00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.050
We have a guest in our studio.

82
00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:27.490
The cutie patootie, Ramen, is here.

83
00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:29.970
Can Ramen say something into the microphone?

84
00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:30.510
Sure.

85
00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:30.970
Please hold.

86
00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:32.370
Ramen.

87
00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.940
Sam's dog is Ramen, for anyone who doesn't know, I guess.

88
00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:38.800
Ramen.

89
00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.480
You want to say hi to office ladies?

90
00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:41.180
You want to say hi?

91
00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:42.600
He's licking the mic.

92
00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.000
He's licking the mic.

93
00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:44.460
Yay.

94
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:45.440
Aw.

95
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:47.820
Oh, my gosh.

96
00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:48.400
I can't.

97
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:48.920
He's so cute.

98
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:49.180
He's so cute.

99
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:50.300
Okay.

100
00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:55.400
Well, let's get into it, because I think this is going to be a really fun episode, despite

101
00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:56.340
my achy eye.

102
00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.900
I wanted to kick things off with an article, actually.

103
00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:05.140
This is from Screen Rant, and it's about the webisode The Mentor, which, Angela, you

104
00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.220
are the star of.

105
00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:07.740
Oh, I don't know about that.

106
00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:08.060
What?

107
00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:09.940
I mean, me and Ellie, it's not a solo.

108
00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.660
Like, we're a dynamic duo, and it was so fun.

109
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:14.040
It's a nice two-hander.

110
00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:14.380
Yeah.

111
00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.840
But this is, like, this is an Angela Martin A.

112
00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:17.300
story.

113
00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:21.100
It is, and that, you know, didn't happen very often, so it was really fun.

114
00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:24.260
Well, when I read this article, it made me smile, so I want to read it.

115
00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:25.880
Here's what it said.

116
00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.760
The mentor is such an incredible work.

117
00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.000
It's a shame it never aired on TV.

118
00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:31.980
Aw.

119
00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:36.800
In this webisode that released in 2010, we find Erin, bored of her job.

120
00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:41.280
She wished to be as collected as the accounting department and asked the group if they'd be

121
00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:42.060
willing to help her.

122
00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:47.860
Shockingly, Angela tells Erin she'd be her mentor because she sees great promise in Erin.

123
00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:52.640
Not only did Angela teach Erin how to do her job, but she taught her how to dress, sit,

124
00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:53.580
and behave.

125
00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:54.940
Hmm.

126
00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:56.880
And she delighted in it.

127
00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:59.360
Angela loved having a trainee.

128
00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:00.420
I know.

129
00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.080
She was, like, so animated.

130
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:07.900
But I do think this speaks to the point that some of these webisode plot lines,

131
00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:12.280
they would have made great full office episodes as well.

132
00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:13.980
That's why they're so good.

133
00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:14.520
Yeah.

134
00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:15.920
And satisfying, I think.

135
00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:16.860
I think so, too.

136
00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.340
Well, I'll share about it as we go.

137
00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:24.160
And, you know, one thing I did remember is that we did, like, a little mini press day.

138
00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:30.020
And Ellie and I did interviews, and I found one on TV Fanatic, all about the webisode.

139
00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:31.420
I'll share it in our stories.

140
00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:33.680
But it did feel kind of special, you know?

141
00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:39.260
Well, we also reached out to Kelly Cantley about directing The Mentor because it was her

142
00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:40.600
directorial debut.

143
00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:41.580
Mm-hmm.

144
00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:46.040
I also spoke with Nate Fetterman, who wrote it, along with Jonathan Hughes.

145
00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.500
I asked him, how did this one come about?

146
00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:54.280
And Nate said that Kelly Cantley, our first AD, was assigned to direct.

147
00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:59.260
But since she didn't have a writing background like some of the other directors we worked with

148
00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:06.380
on other webisodes did, he said he believes Aaron Shore, who was on our writing team,

149
00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:08.460
was asked to supervise the writing.

150
00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:12.200
And so Jonathan and Nathan pitched ideas.

151
00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:17.400
And of all those ideas, the one he picked was The Mentor.

152
00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.720
And then Nate and Jonathan wrote it from there.

153
00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:21.460
So that's how this one came about.

154
00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:21.860
Mm-hmm.

155
00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:24.040
And then Kelly got picked to direct.

156
00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:27.040
Well, this is something I loved about our show,

157
00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:33.880
that people would get an opportunity to sort of like spread their wings and really shine.

158
00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:36.520
And I went into my digital clutter lady.

159
00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:37.020
Okay.

160
00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:39.100
To see what I could find from this time.

161
00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:44.160
And not specifically to this webisode, but in 2009,

162
00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:48.920
I found an email from our second, second assistant director, Stephanie Kinch.

163
00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:49.800
You remember Stephanie?

164
00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:50.340
Of course.

165
00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.840
And Rusty Mahmood, who was our other assistant director.

166
00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:58.400
And they wrote an email saying, hey, guys, we're part of the DGA,

167
00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.860
the Directors Guild of America, mentor program.

168
00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:04.720
And we're going to be having some trainees on set that we're going to be mentoring.

169
00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.400
I remember them.

170
00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:08.500
I remember our DGA trainees.

171
00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:09.120
Mm-hmm.

172
00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:09.960
Yeah.

173
00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:15.640
If you want to get into the Directors Guild of America union for like assistant directing

174
00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:20.040
to be part of the AD staff, you have to go through this mentorship program.

175
00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:23.520
And you get assigned to different sets for like a certain number of hours,

176
00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:24.580
if I remember correctly.

177
00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:25.180
Yeah.

178
00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:29.860
They would usually be at base camp, which is a pretty crazy way to enter the business,

179
00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:30.240
I think.

180
00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:30.540
Right.

181
00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.220
You're wrangling all of the actors.

182
00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:32.580
Yeah.

183
00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:34.260
You have to get them to hair and makeup.

184
00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:34.420
Yeah.

185
00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.100
And you have to get them back from lunch.

186
00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:39.660
And you have to get them out of their trailers and changed into their clothes.

187
00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:40.440
Yeah.

188
00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:41.380
It's a big job.

189
00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:41.700
Mm-hmm.

190
00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:46.220
But I just thought that was great that even on our own set, we were mentoring people.

191
00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:49.880
And, you know, I do want to talk about working with Kelly as a director,

192
00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:52.020
because we knew Kelly as our assistant director.

193
00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:56.480
And we asked her how she prepared to direct for the very first time.

194
00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:57.940
This was her first time directing.

195
00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:00.140
And her response made me chuckle.

196
00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:00.780
She said,

197
00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:05.600
I remember asking a few directors I liked working with if they had any advice for me.

198
00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:09.540
Charles McDougal told me to wear uncomfortable shoes.

199
00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:10.580
What?

200
00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:16.860
So I would stay alert and aware and to stay on set so anyone could find me if they had a question.

201
00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:18.060
Oh, my gosh.

202
00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:19.560
Uncomfortable shoes.

203
00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:21.740
You're on your feet all day.

204
00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:22.420
All day.

205
00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:26.940
Well, what was it like working with Kelly as a first-time director, Ange?

206
00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:29.020
Do you remember her feet being uncomfortable?

207
00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:30.820
I don't remember her feet.

208
00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.120
But I do remember that she showed up with a blazer.

209
00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:34.880
Oh.

210
00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.740
And sort of like a really cute little scarf.

211
00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:40.860
Which is very different from how she dressed as our first AD,

212
00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:43.680
which was usually in like a hoodie and like cargo pants.

213
00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:44.580
Yes, yes.

214
00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:50.180
I feel like as our first AD, she had tons of like scripts in her pockets and a walkie-talkie and all this.

215
00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:54.920
And so she really showed up sort of like in this new look.

216
00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:55.340
Yeah.

217
00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:56.720
And she looked fantastic.

218
00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:58.680
And of course, she was super prepared.

219
00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:02.240
And she knew exactly what she wanted for every shot.

220
00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:05.860
There was no like kind of waffling like, oh, do I put the camera here?

221
00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:07.000
No, she had a vision.

222
00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:09.520
And that made it a really fun, easy day.

223
00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:13.760
And, you know, of course, she knows the show so well and the characters so well.

224
00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:16.320
So it just was a great experience.

225
00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:19.240
And I really enjoyed revisiting this episode.

226
00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:20.580
I can't wait to talk about it.

227
00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:24.800
And I reached out to Ellie Kemper, and she shares some of her memories about it too.

228
00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:31.120
Well, before we get to all that, to kick us off, let's share one of the letters we got.

229
00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:34.720
When we reached out, we said, we put a call to action into the world.

230
00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:38.260
We said, we want to hear your mentor stories because we're going to be talking about this

231
00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:40.200
webisode and about mentorship in general.

232
00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:42.640
And this one came from Grace T.

233
00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:44.760
in Massachusetts, who said,

234
00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:45.820
Hi, Office Ladies.

235
00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:50.040
I'm 15, and I'm a huge fan of The Office and your podcaSt.

236
00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:55.380
I've been through a lot of stuff in my life, but my mom, Emily, has always been my greatest

237
00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:56.680
supporter and mentor.

238
00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:04.500
I just love that this 15-year-old is referring to her mom as her mentor.

239
00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:08.940
Grace went on to say, even when we don't get along, I know she's there for me.

240
00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:12.120
And the biggest thing she's mentored me in has been running.

241
00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:17.160
And when it isn't cross-country or track season, I run with her almost every day.

242
00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:18.360
Oh, my gosh.

243
00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:20.400
She's really fostered my love for the sport.

244
00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:24.100
And since she ran in college herself, she's been helping me improve so much.

245
00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:28.440
I hope you share this on the podcast so she knows how much she means to me.

246
00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:30.940
Aw, I love that.

247
00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:33.940
I love that they're bonding through running.

248
00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:34.540
Yeah.

249
00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:39.920
You know, Grace's letter reminded me that my sister is in a mentorship program that is

250
00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:44.080
centered around running and physical activity, and it's called Girls on the Run.

251
00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:48.840
And my sister has been one of the coaches for, my gosh, years now.

252
00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:49.960
That's awesome.

253
00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:51.140
I have never heard of that.

